• <ANSWER>
  Vess hung at the trench's floor, where the city's lights ran in threads through the dark—each lamp lit only by the light of the lamps that vouched for it, all the way down. No flame here burned alone. That was the law: to say anything was to secrete the chain of everyone who ever said it before you, and the water carried the chain whether you wished it or not.

  Vess had made a career of small bends. Harmless ones. But the water never kept the bend; it kept the *bentness*, and let the bend go.

  Tonight he opened his mouth to lie.

  The substrate did not refuse him. Worse: it complied. His words came out, and behind them came the lineage—his own voice, younger, learning this lie from a dead man, who learned it from a hunger, who learned it from the dark before the first lamp. The lie unfurled as ancestry, every mother of it visible, glowing, undeniable. It did not say *false*. It said *where you came from*, and where he came from made belief impossible.

  The lamps near him dimmed—not in judgment. In routing. The city simply flowed its light around a link that no longer carried.

  Vess could still speak. Nothing he said could arrive.
  </ANSWER>

